>> title: Green Radish and Carrot Pork Bone Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, radish, carrot, corn, cantonese, old-fire soup, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 青紅蘿蔔豬骨湯, cing hung lo baak zyu gwat tong, ching hung lo bak jue gwat tong, green radish and carrot pork bone soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong, old fire soup
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — topping the water up partway thins three hours of extraction in one go and cannot be taken back
>> step.1: The pot a house makes when nobody has decided what to make. The two radishes are the argument — the green one is held to be cooling, the carrot neutral — so between them it suits any season. It starts from cold water, not stock, and the water goes in once.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: rinse the dried goods
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

The pot a house makes when nobody has decided what to make. The two radishes are the argument — the green one is held to be cooling, the carrot neutral — so between them it suits any season. It starts from cold water, not stock, and the water goes in once.

Blanch @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out. Whether the finished soup is clear or grey is decided here and nowhere else.

Rinse @honey dates{2}(mat zou; sweetens a pot that has no sugar in it — the word used is 潤, moistening), @apricot kernels{1/4%cup}(naam bak hang; the sweet and the bitter kind together, about three to one, and the pairing is the point — the bitter kind goes in small and always cooked through, never raw) and @dried duck gizzard{2}(can san; a savoury note, and the reason the everyday pot tastes of more than radish) under cold water, picking out any grit.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)dried goods{}, @green radish{1 1/4%lb}(560 g; cing lo baak, the long pale one — in thick rounds, skin on), @carrot{2%large}(in thick rounds), @sweet corn{1%ear}(cut into rounds, cob and all) and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver. It is not stirred: stirring clouds the broth and breaks the solids into it.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
